# Copy to deploy/cloudflared.env (chmod 600) and set the tunnel token.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# The Cloudflare tunnel credential is a bearer token: anything holding it can
# run a connector for this tunnel. It must not live in .env.production, because
# every service in docker-compose.prod.yml inherits that file through
# `env_file:` - so a secret added there changes the main stack's compose config
# hash and can recreate both API colours on the next `up -d`. That is the same
# trap deploy/alert.env.example documents for ALERT_EMAIL.
#
# deploy/cloudflared.env is gitignored and is read only by
# deploy/docker-compose.cloudflared.yml, via --env-file.
#
# WHERE TO GET THE TOKEN
# ----------------------
# Cloudflare Zero Trust dashboard -> Networks -> Tunnels -> the tunnel ->
# "Install and run a connector" -> Docker. It is the long `eyJ...` string after
# `--token`. The dashboard will not show it again afterwards, and the running
# container's copy is the only other place it is stored, so keep this file.
#
# One token may back more than one connector instance - that is how the tunnel
# is run in HA across two hosts. Each instance shows up separately under the
# tunnel in the dashboard; do not mint a second token for the second host.
#
# TO ROTATE: roll the connector token in the dashboard, update this file, then
#   docker compose -f deploy/docker-compose.cloudflared.yml --env-file deploy/cloudflared.env up -d

TUNNEL_TOKEN=
